{"id":"523e8276-cc0a-4224-8e5c-23a0e7efde11","object":"chat.completion","created":1785018587,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    required_keys = ['word_token_indices', 'word_scores', 'forced_words', 'token_budget']\\n    for k in required_keys:\\n        if k not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required key: {k}\\\")\\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\\n    if not isinstance(word_token_indices, list) or not isinstance(word_scores, list) or not isinstance(forced_words, list) or not isinstance(token_budget, int):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid types\\\")\\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_scores):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Mismatched lengths\\\")\\n    for i, tokens in enumerate(word_token_indices):\\n        if not isinstance(tokens, list):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"word_token_indices[{i}] not a list\\\")\\n        for t in tokens:\\n            if not isinstance(t, int):\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Token index {t} not integer\\\")\\n    for s in word_scores:\\n        if not isinstance(s, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Score not numeric\\\")\\n    for idx in forced_words:\\n        if not isinstance(idx, int):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Forced word index not integer\\\")\\n        if idx < 0 or idx >= len(word_token_indices):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Forced word index out of range\\\")\\n    if token_budget < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Token budget negative\\\")\\n    lens = [len(t) for t in word_token_indices]\\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\\n    forced_tokens = sum(lens[i] for i in forced_set)\\n    if forced_tokens > token_budget:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Forced words exceed token budget\\\")\\n    remaining = token_budget - forced_tokens\\n    candidates = [(word_scores[i], i) for i in range(len(word_scores)) if i not in forced_set]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\\n    selected = set(forced_set)\\n    for score, idx in candidates:\\n        if lens[idx] <= remaining:\\n            selected.add(idx)\\n            remaining -= lens[idx]\\n    selected_words = sorted(selected)\\n    retained_token_indices = []\\n    for idx in selected_words:\\n        retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\\n    tokens_used = token_budget - remaining\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_token_indices\\\": retained_token_indices,\\n        \\\"selected_words\\\": selected_words,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": tokens_used\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) according to the task description and output schema. The task is NLP-LL2-02: budgeted token reconstruction. Input case payload has word_token_indices (list of lists of token indices), word_scores (list of floats), forced_words (list of word indices that must be included), token_budget (integer). Output must include retained_token_indices (list of integers: token indices that are retained), selected_words (list of integers: word indices that are selected), tokens_used (integer: total number of tokens used). The algorithm: forced words are selected first; if total tokens of forced words exceed budget, raise ValueError. Then among remaining words, sort by descending score, and for ties by source position (presumably original index order). For each candidate word, if its token count (len of its token indices list) fits within remaining budget, include it. Continue until no more fit. Selected words must be returned in original order (i.e., the order they appear in the input word list, not sorted). The retained_token_indices should be the token indices of all selected words, flattened and sorted? The spec says \"Return words and tokens in original order with exact token usage.\" For tokens, \"retained_token_indices\": list of integers. It should be the token indices that are retained. Since tokens are from selected words, and we want them in original order? Probably meaning the order as they appear in the original sequence? The tokens are globally indexed. The input word_token_indices are given in order of words. So if we select words in original order, the token indices should be concatenated in that order to get the sequence. So retained_token_indices should be the concatenation of the token index lists of selected words, in the order of selected_words (which is original order). That makes sense.\n\nCheck fixtures: For case b-000, word_token_indices:\n0: [0,1,2]\n1: [3,4,5]\n2: [6,7]\n3: [8,9]\n4: [10,11]\n5: [12]\nword_scores: [0.5616647, 0.67600376, 0.13305957, 0.25496266, 0.15449714, 0.3877475]\nforced_words: [0]\ntoken_budget: 6\nForced word 0 uses 3 tokens, remaining budget 3. Remaining words sorted by score descending, then position:\nWord1 score 0.67600376 (index1), word5 score 0.3877475 (index5), word3 score 0.25496266 (index3), word4 score 0.15449714 (index4), word2 score 0.13305957 (index2).\nCheck each:\nword1 (index1): tokens [3,4,5] length 3 <= 3 -> select, remaining 0.\nword5 tokens [12] length 1 > 0 -> skip.\nOthers skip.\nSo selected words: forced word0, then word1 => indices [0,1] in original order. total tokens used = 3+3=6. retained_token_indices = [0,1,2,3,4,5]. Output matches expected? Not given, but we'll compute.\n\nCase b-001: word_token_indices:\n0: [0,1,2]\n1: [3,4,5]\n2: [6,7]\n3: [8]\n4: [9,10]\n5: [11]\nscores: [0.4030323, 0.92794295, 0.98830093, 0.10996368, 0.09172307, 0.38023833]\nforced: [0]\nbudget 6. Forced uses 3 tokens, remaining 3.\nSorted remaining by score descending: word2 (0.98830093, index2), word1 (0.92794295, index1), word5 (0.38023833, index5), word3 (0.10996368, index3), word4 (0.09172307, index4).\nword2 tokens [6,7] length 2 <=3 -> select, remaining 1.\nword1 tokens [3,4,5] length 3 >1 -> skip.\nword5 tokens [11] length 1 <=1 -> select, remaining 0.\nThen word3 tokens [8] length 1 >0 skip.\nSelected words in order: 0,2,5 -> indices [0,2,5]. tokens used: 3+2+1=6. retained tokens: [0,1,2,6,7,11]. Output accordingly.\n\nCase b-002: word_token_indices:\n0: [0,1,2]\n1: [3]\n2: [4,5]\n3: [6]\n4: [7,8,9]\n5: [10]\nscores: [0.21499785, 0.31970575, 0.12422725, 0.45165188, 0.13756562, 0.05851525]\nforced: [0]\nbudget 5. Forced uses 3 tokens, remaining 2.\nSorted remaining: word3 (0.45165188, index3), word1 (0.31970575, index1), word0 is forced already, word2 (0.12422725, index2), word4 (0.13756562, index4), word5 (0.05851525, index5).\nword3 tokens [6] length 1 <=2 -> select, remaining 1.\nword1 tokens [3] length 1 <=1 -> select, remaining 0.\nNext word2 tokens [4,5] length 2 >0 skip.\nSelected words in order: forced0, word3 (index3), word1 (index1) -> but need original order: index0, index1, index3? Wait original indices: 0,1,2,3,4,5. Selected: 0 (forced), then we selected word3 (index3) and word1 (index1) from sorted order. But we must return selected_words in original order, i.e., sorted by index. So we need to collect selected indices and then sort them by index (original position). That's what \"original order\" means: the order of words as they appear in the input. So selected_words should be sorted by index. So indices: 0,1,3. tokens used: 3+1+1=5. retained_token_indices: concatenation of word0[0,1,2], word1[3], word3[6] => [0,1,2,3,6]. That seems correct.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate inputs: case must have required fields? According to task, we should validate every required field before computing. The payload must have word_token_indices, word_scores, forced_words, token_budget. If missing, raise ValueError. Also ensure types are correct (list of lists, list of numbers, list of ints, int). Also token_budget >=0, forced_words indices within range, etc. For simplicity, we can assume valid per fixtures, but to be safe we can validate.\n\n2. Extract data: indices_list = case['word_token_indices'], scores = case['word_scores'], forced = case['forced_words'], budget = case['token_budget'].\n\n3. Compute token lengths: lens = [len(t) for t in indices_list].\n\n4. Check forced: total forced tokens = sum(lens[i] for i in forced). If > budget, raise ValueError(\"Forced words exceed token budget\").\n\n5. Initialize selected set of indices (for quick lookup), selected ordered list (to output in original order). Start with forced words added to selected set and ordered list (but need to ensure no duplicates and order? forced words list may have duplicates? Should not, but we can handle). Forced words must be added in the order they appear in forced list? The spec says \"Select forced words first\" but doesn't specify order among forced. Since they are forced, we include them all. But the output selected_words should be in original order (by index), so the order doesn't matter as we will sort later. So we add all forced indices to set and later we will sort them.\n\n6. Compute remaining budget = budget - sum(lens[i] for i in forced). If remaining budget <0, error.\n\n7. Build list of candidate indices: all word indices not in forced, each with score and index. Sort by (-score, index) for descending score, then ascending index for ties.\n\n8. Iterate over candidates: for each idx, if lens[idx] <= remaining, select it: add to set, decrease remaining, continue. Else skip.\n\n9. Build selected_words list: sorted(selected_set) to get original order (ascending index). That matches \"original order\".\n\n10. Build retained_token_indices: for idx in selected_words (sorted), extend the list with indices_list[idx] (the actual token indices). Ensure they are integers.\n\n11. tokens_used = total budget - remaining (or sum of lens of selected). Compute integer.\n\n12. Return dict with keys: retained_token_indices, selected_words, tokens_used.\n\nEdge cases: If forced words list empty, just start with empty selected set. If no words fit after forced, selected_words may be only forced. If budget is exactly enough for forced and maybe additional, fine. If some forced words have duplicate indices? Not, but if duplicate in forced list, treat as duplicate inclusion? Better to assume no duplicates, but we can deduplicate by using set.\n\nAlso note: The spec says \"Return words and tokens in original order with exact token usage.\" So we need to output selected_words as list of integers (word indices) in increasing order (original order). That's fine.\n\nNow, write the solve function. It should be Python source code. Return the JSON object with implementation key.\n\nWe must ensure we don't import anything beyond standard library (math, etc. are allowed but not needed). Use only built-in types.\n\nWe need to handle the case where forced_words may be provided as list or maybe empty. Also token_budget could be large.\n\nWe must raise ValueError for infeasible (forced too many tokens) or structurally invalid. Also we could check that lengths of indices_list and scores match, etc.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate required keys\n    required_keys = ['word_token_indices', 'word_scores', 'forced_words', 'token_budget']\n    for k in required_keys:\n        if k not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing required key: {k}\")\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\n\n    # Validate types and consistency\n    if not isinstance(word_token_indices, list) or not isinstance(word_scores, list) or not isinstance(forced_words, list) or not isinstance(token_budget, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid types\")\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_scores):\n        raise ValueError(\"Mismatched lengths\")\n    # Validate each word_token_indices element is list of ints, each word_scores element is numeric, etc.\n    for i, tokens in enumerate(word_token_indices):\n        if not isinstance(tokens, list):\n            raise ValueError(f\"word_token_indices[{i}] not a list\")\n        for t in tokens:\n            if not isinstance(t, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"Token index {t} not integer\")\n    for s in word_scores:\n        if not isinstance(s, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"Score not numeric\")\n    for idx in forced_words:\n        if not isinstance(idx, int):\n            raise ValueError(\"Forced word index not integer\")\n        if idx < 0 or idx >= len(word_token_indices):\n            raise ValueError(\"Forced word index out of range\")\n    if token_budget < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Token budget negative\")\n\n    # Compute lengths\n    lens = [len(t) for t in word_token_indices]\n\n    # Forced tokens\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\n    forced_tokens = sum(lens[i] for i in forced_set)\n    if forced_tokens > token_budget:\n        raise ValueError(\"Forced words exceed token budget\")\n    remaining = token_budget - forced_tokens\n\n    # Build candidate list: (score, index) for indices not forced\n    candidates = [(word_scores[i], i) for i in range(len(word_scores)) if i not in forced_set]\n    # Sort descending score, then ascending index\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\n\n    selected = set(forced_set)  # set for quick membership\n    for score, idx in candidates:\n        if lens[idx] <= remaining:\n            selected.add(idx)\n            remaining -= lens[idx]\n\n    # Build output in original order (sorted indices)\n    selected_words = sorted(selected)\n    retained_token_indices = []\n    for idx in selected_words:\n        retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\n    tokens_used = token_budget - remaining  # or sum(lens[i] for i in selected)\n    # Ensure tokens_used is int\n    tokens_used = int(tokens_used)\n\n    return {\n        \"retained_token_indices\": retained_token_indices,\n        \"selected_words\": selected_words,\n        \"tokens_used\": tokens_used\n    }\n\nThat's it.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON with key \"implementation\" and value being the source code as a JSON-escaped string. So we must serialize the Python source code into a JSON string. We have to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. We'll manually construct the JSON or use json.dumps in our mind. Since we are writing the response, we need to produce the final JSON object.\n\nWe'll write the Python code as a single string with proper escaping. We'll use triple-quotes? Actually need to escape for JSON. Let's write the code in a formatted way but then escape. Better to write the code in a clean way and then replace newlines with \\n, backslashes with \\\\, quotes with \\\". We'll do it manually.\n\nImplementation code (without indentation issues) - we'll indent with 4 spaces.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    required_keys = ['word_token_indices', 'word_scores', 'forced_words', 'token_budget']\\n    for k in required_keys:\\n        if k not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required key: {k}\\\")\\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\\n    if not isinstance(word_token_indices, list) or not isinstance(word_scores, list) or not isinstance(forced_words, list) or not isinstance(token_budget, int):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid types\\\")\\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_scores):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Mismatched lengths\\\")\\n    for i, tokens in enumerate(word_token_indices):\\n        if not isinstance(tokens, list):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"word_token_indices[{i}] not a list\\\")\\n        for t in tokens:\\n            if not isinstance(t, int):\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Token index {t} not integer\\\")\\n    for s in word_scores:\\n        if not isinstance(s, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Score not numeric\\\")\\n    for idx in forced_words:\\n        if not isinstance(idx, int):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Forced word index not integer\\\")\\n        if idx < 0 or idx >= len(word_token_indices):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Forced word index out of range\\\")\\n    if token_budget < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Token budget negative\\\")\\n    lens = [len(t) for t in word_token_indices]\\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\\n    forced_tokens = sum(lens[i] for i in forced_set)\\n    if forced_tokens > token_budget:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Forced words exceed token budget\\\")\\n    remaining = token_budget - forced_tokens\\n    candidates = [(word_scores[i], i) for i in range(len(word_scores)) if i not in forced_set]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\\n    selected = set(forced_set)\\n    for score, idx in candidates:\\n        if lens[idx] <= remaining:\\n            selected.add(idx)\\n            remaining -= lens[idx]\\n    selected_words = sorted(selected)\\n    retained_token_indices = []\\n    for idx in selected_words:\\n        retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\\n    tokens_used = token_budget - remaining\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_token_indices\\\": retained_token_indices,\\n        \\\"selected_words\\\": selected_words,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": tokens_used\\n    }\"\n}\n\nBut we need to ensure the string is properly escaped. For example, inside the code, there are double quotes. We need to escape them with backslashes. Also we have f-strings with curly braces, those are fine because they are inside the string content. However, when embedding in JSON, we need to escape backslashes and quotes. So we'll write the code as a raw string then escape.\n\nI'll produce the JSON manually, ensuring all escapes are correct. Let's write the code in a text block then convert.\n\nAlternatively, I can use a JSON encoder mentally. Since the response must be a single JSON object, I'll write it directly with escaped characters.\n\nI'll start with \"{\"implementation\": \" and then the code with each double quote inside replaced by \\\", each backslash by \\\\, and newline by \\n.\n\nI'll copy the code and perform replacements.\n\nLet me write the code with explicit \\n and \\\" inside.\n\nThe code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    required_keys = ['word_token_indices', 'word_scores', 'forced_words', 'token_budget']\n    for k in required_keys:\n        if k not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing required key: {k}\")\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\n    if not isinstance(word_token_indices, list) or not isinstance(word_scores, list) or not isinstance(forced_words, list) or not isinstance(token_budget, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid types\")\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_scores):\n        raise ValueError(\"Mismatched lengths\")\n    for i, tokens in enumerate(word_token_indices):\n        if not isinstance(tokens, list):\n            raise ValueError(f\"word_token_indices[{i}] not a list\")\n        for t in tokens:\n            if not isinstance(t, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"Token index {t} not integer\")\n    for s in word_scores:\n        if not isinstance(s, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"Score not numeric\")\n    for idx in forced_words:\n        if not isinstance(idx, int):\n            raise ValueError(\"Forced word index not integer\")\n        if idx < 0 or idx >= len(word_token_indices):\n            raise ValueError(\"Forced word index out of range\")\n    if token_budget < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Token budget negative\")\n    lens = [len(t) for t in word_token_indices]\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\n    forced_tokens = sum(lens[i] for i in forced_set)\n    if forced_tokens > token_budget:\n        raise ValueError(\"Forced words exceed token budget\")\n    remaining = token_budget - forced_tokens\n    candidates = [(word_scores[i], i) for i in range(len(word_scores)) if i not in forced_set]\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\n    selected = set(forced_set)\n    for score, idx in candidates:\n        if lens[idx] <= remaining:\n            selected.add(idx)\n            remaining -= lens[idx]\n    selected_words = sorted(selected)\n    retained_token_indices = []\n    for idx in selected_words:\n        retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\n    tokens_used = token_budget - remaining\n    return {\n        \"retained_token_indices\": retained_token_indices,\n        \"selected_words\": selected_words,\n        \"tokens_used\": tokens_used\n    }\n\nNow escape for JSON:\n- Replace each backslash with double backslash: but there are no backslashes in the code except in f-strings? Actually there is no backslash. So only need to escape double quotes and newlines.\n- Each double quote inside the string (e.g., 'word_token_indices' is single-quoted, but there are double quotes in the return dict and in raise strings like \"Invalid types\". Those double quotes must be escaped as \\\". Also the f-string uses double quotes for the key? f\"Missing required key: {k}\" - the outer is double quote, so escape that.\n- Newline characters become \\n.\n\nI'll produce the escaped version line by line, but easier: I'll write the whole string with explicit escapes.\n\nLet me construct:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    required_keys = ['word_token_indices', 'word_scores', 'forced_words', 'token_budget']\\n    for k in required_keys:\\n        if k not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required key: {k}\\\")\\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\\n    if not isinstance(word_token_indices, list) or not isinstance(word_scores, list) or not isinstance(forced_words, list) or not isinstance(token_budget, int):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid types\\\")\\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_scores):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Mismatched lengths\\\")\\n    for i, tokens in enumerate(word_token_indices):\\n        if not isinstance(tokens, list):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"word_token_indices[{i}] not a list\\\")\\n        for t in tokens:\\n            if not isinstance(t, int):\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Token index {t} not integer\\\")\\n    for s in word_scores:\\n        if not isinstance(s, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Score not numeric\\\")\\n    for idx in forced_words:\\n        if not isinstance(idx, int):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Forced word index not integer\\\")\\n        if idx < 0 or idx >= len(word_token_indices):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Forced word index out of range\\\")\\n    if token_budget < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Token budget negative\\\")\\n    lens = [len(t) for t in word_token_indices]\\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\\n    forced_tokens = sum(lens[i] for i in forced_set)\\n    if forced_tokens > token_budget:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Forced words exceed token budget\\\")\\n    remaining = token_budget - forced_tokens\\n    candidates = [(word_scores[i], i) for i in range(len(word_scores)) if i not in forced_set]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\\n    selected = set(forced_set)\\n    for score, idx in candidates:\\n        if lens[idx] <= remaining:\\n            selected.add(idx)\\n            remaining -= lens[idx]\\n    selected_words = sorted(selected)\\n    retained_token_indices = []\\n    for idx in selected_words:\\n        retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\\n    tokens_used = token_budget - remaining\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retai